<node id="Tau Pathology and Neurodegeneration">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Hyperphosphorylated tau (p-tau) accumulates intracellularly, forming neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs). This disrupts axonal transport, leading to neuronal dysfunction and death. Tau pathology correlates strongly with the degree of cognitive impairment and brain atrophy. CSF tau levels (TAU, PTAU) are key biomarkers.</data>
  <data key="d2">45</data>
</node>
<node id="TAU">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="PTAU">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="MidTemp">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="Ventricles">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="WholeBrain">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="Fusiform">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="Synaptic Dysfunction and Loss">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Early in AD, synaptic connections are damaged and lost, even before significant neuronal death. This impairs communication between neurons and contributes significantly to cognitive decline. Both Aβ oligomers and tau pathology contribute to synapse loss.</data>
  <data key="d2">37</data>
</node>
<node id="Neuroinflammation">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Microglia and astrocytes become activated in response to Aβ plaques and NFTs. This neuroinflammatory response, while initially protective, can become chronic and contribute to neuronal damage and disease progression.</data>
  <data key="d2">37</data>
</node>
<node id="Glucose Hypometabolism">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Reduced glucose uptake in specific brain regions, particularly the temporoparietal cortex, is characteristic of AD. This reflects impaired neuronal function and energy metabolism.</data>
  <data key="d2">38</data>
</node>
<node id="FDG">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="Brain Atrophy and Structural Changes">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Progressive loss of brain tissue volume, particularly in the hippocampus, entorhinal cortex, and later widespread cortical regions. This reflects neuronal death and synapse loss, contributing to cognitive decline.</data>
  <data key="d2">44</data>
</node>
<node id="ICV">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="Cognitive Impairment Progression">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Decline in various cognitive domains (memory, executive function, attention, language, visuospatial skills) as a hallmark of AD progression and diagnosis.</data>
  <data key="d2">39</data>
</node>
<node id="CDRSB.1">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
